  "data": {
    "accession": "PXD059888",
    "title": "Structural Insights into Complement Inhibition: Visualizing Distinct Binding Modes of C4b-binding Protein Complexes with C4b and SAP",
    "additionalAttributes": [],
    "projectDescription": "C4b-binding protein (C4BP) is an innate immune inhibitor found in serum. Human C4BP adopts spider-like higher-order structures (HOS) formed by disulfide-linked C4BPα and C4BPβ chains that non-covalently bind vitamin K-dependent protein S (ProS). These spider-like structures can form even larger complexes as C4BP interacts with other, mostly complement-related, proteins. The complement inhibitory role of C4BP is primarily mediated through its interaction with C4b. C4BP also binds with high affinity to serum amyloid P component (SAP), a pentraxin family member associated with amyloidosis conditions. Here, we structurally and compositionally characterize C4BP interactions with these two natively occurring binders. To achieve this, we combine mass photometry, high-speed atomic force microscopy, and cross-linking mass spectrometry. By integrating the results, we reveal two distinct binding modes of C4BP when bound to C4b or SAP. Given the spider-like assembly of C4BP, C4b interacts with the N-terminal region of a single C4BPα leg, enabling multiple C4b molecules to bind to the C4BP HOS. Conversely, SAP engages with the entire spider-like HOS: the C4BPα-C4BPβ oligomerization core binds to the B-face of SAP, and the C4BPα legs wrap around it.",
    "sampleProcessingProtocol": "The experiments were performed using human plasma-purified protein samples (C4BP and C4b) and recombinantly expressed proteins (C4BPα and SAP) were used. The C4BP and C4b samples (Complement Technology, Inc) were purified from healthy, pooled donor plasma of more than 16 donors. Bis(2,5-dioxopyrrolidin-1-yl) glutarate (DSG), 1-(2,5-Dioxocyclopentyl) 8-(2,5-dioxopyrrolidin-1-yl) octanedioate (DSS), and bis(2,5-Dioxopyrrolidin-1-yl) 3,3'-sulfinyldipropionate (DSSO) were acquired from CF Plus Chemicals. The C4BP-C4b were prepared by incubating C4BP or C4BPα 0.1 mg/mL (final concentration) with C4b to achieve 1:1 C4BP:C4b or 2:1 C4BPα:C4b molar ratio (37 °C, 1h, 350 rpm) in 50 mM HEPES, pH = 7.4, 150 mM NaCl. The C4BP-SAP complexes were prepared in a similar manner as the C4BP complexes with C4b. C4BP 0.1 mg/mL (final concentration) was incubated with SAP to achieve 2:1 SAP:C4BP molar ratio (37 °C, 1h, 350 rpm) in 50 mM HEPES, pH = 7.4, 50 mM NaCl, 1 mM CaCl2. Cross-linking was performed in an experimental triplicate with DSS, DSG, and DSSO, all at 0.5 mM concentration. The addition of a crosslinker resulted in a final DMSO concentration of 5% (v/v) in the sample. Immediately after the 1 h incubation, the reaction was quenched with 1 M Tris (pH = 8.5) to achieve a final concentration of 50 mM in the sample. The cross-linked samples were denatured by 8 M urea. Reduction and alkylation were performed with 10 mM TCEP and 40 mM CAA (30 min, room temperature (RT)). After, the samples were diluted with N-ethylmpoline buffer (pH = 8.5), to reduce the urea concentration to 2 M. The samples were digested by trypsin (1:20 (w/w)) and LysC (1:50 (w/w)) for 16h, 37 °C. The digestion was stopped by TFA addition (final 0.5% (v/v)), and the resulting peptides were desalted on an Oasis HLB µElution Plate (Waters Corp) using a vendor-provided protocol. Solvent from the desalted peptides was fully evaporated, and the peptides were deglycosylated using a Protein Deglycosylation Mix II (New England Biolabs) according to a vendor-provided protocol. The deglycosylated peptides were desalted, and the solvent was fully evaporated. Prior to the mass spectrometry (MS) analysis, the peptides were dissolved in 2% (v/v) FA.  The liquid chromatography (LC)-MS/MS analysis was performed on an UltiMate 3000 UHPLC system coupled to an Orbitrap Exploris 480 (Thermo Fisher Scientific Inc). The cross-linked peptides were analyzed in a 90 min gradient. First, the peptides were trapped on an Acclaim Pepmap 100 C18 (5 mm × 0.3 mm, 5 μm, Thermo Fisher Scientific Inc) for 1 min at a flow rate of 30 μL/min, solvent A (0.1% FA v/v in H2O). The trapping was followed by a separation on an in-house packed analytical column (Reprosil 2.4 μm, 75 μm × 50 cm) at a flow rate of 0.3 μL/min using a gradient starting from 9% solvent B (80% acetonitrile (ACN) v/v, 0.1% FA v/v) at 0–1 min, 13% B at 2 min; 44% B at 67 min, 55% B 72 min, 99% B 75 min, and equilibrated for the next run in 9% B at 80–90 min. MS1 scans were collected with the following parameters: resolution = 60000, mass range = 375-1600 Th, automatic AGC target and maximum injection time, cycle time = 1s. MS2 scans were collected in data-dependent mode for precursors with charge state above 3, with the following parameters: isolation window = 1.4 Th, resolution = 30000, mass range = 200-2000 Th. Precursors that were once selected for fragmentation were excluded from MS2 for 30 seconds. The peptides were fragmented using stHCD with NCE 19, 25, 31% for DSSO, and NCE 24, 28, 33% for DSG and DSS.",
    "dataProcessingProtocol": "The resulting raw spectra were searched using pLink 2 against a FASTA file containing common contaminants and the most abundant proteins with IBAQ >9.5 × 106 from the C4BP sample, as identified by bottom-up proteomics. Additionally, the C4A and C4B genes were divided into three separate chains (α, β, and γ), which form the C4b structure. Propeptides and signal peptides were removed from the FASTA file, and SAP sequence was added. In the case of the C4b complex with recombinant C4BPα, the C4BPα sequence was substituted by the recombinant protein sequence. The linker mass cross-linking two lysine residues was set to be 96.021 Da, 138.068 Da, and 158.004 Da for DSG, DSS, and DSSO, respectively. Trypsin was set as the enzyme, cleaving at the C-terminal of K and R with up to 3 allowed missed cleavages. The peptide mass and length were set to be between 600-6000 Da and 6-60 amino acids, respectively. Precursor and fragment tolerances were set to 20 ppm. Carbamidomethylation [C] was set as a fixed modification, deamidation [N], and oxidation [M] were allowed as variable modifications. Results were filtered for 1% FDR, a score lower than 10e-4, and cross-links present in at least 2 out of 3 replicates.",
